.\" $OpenBSD$
.\"
.Dd $Mdocdate: May 5 2026 $
.Dt URTWX 4
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm urtwx
.Nd Realtek RTL8821CU USB IEEE 802.11a/ac/b/g/n wireless network device
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Cd "urtwx* at uhub? port ?"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
driver supports USB wireless network devices based on the Realtek RTL8821CU
chipset.
.Pp
The RTL8821CU is a highly integrated dual-band 802.11ac adapter that combines
a MAC, a 1T1R capable baseband, an RF, and a USB interface in a single chip.
It can operate in the 2GHz and 5GHz bands.
.Pp
The
.Nm
driver supports 802.11n high throughput (HT) operation, including 40MHz
channels in both the 2GHz and 5GHz bands, and 802.11ac very high throughput
(VHT) operation with 80MHz channels in the 5GHz band on non-DFS channels.
.Pp
The
.Nm
driver can be configured to use
Wired Equivalent Privacy (WEP) or
Wi-Fi Protected Access (WPA1 and WPA2).
WPA2 is currently the most secure encryption standard for wireless networks
supported by
.Ox .
It is strongly recommended that neither WEP nor WPA1
are used as the sole mechanism to secure wireless communication,
due to serious weaknesses.
WPA1 is disabled by default and may be enabled using the option
.Qq Cm wpaprotos Ar wpa1,wpa2 .
For standard WPA networks which use pre-shared keys (PSK),
keys are configured using the
.Qq Cm wpakey
option.
WPA-Enterprise networks require use of the wpa_supplicant package.
The
.Nm
driver offloads encryption and decryption of data frames to the hardware for
the CCMP cipher.
.Pp
The
.Nm
driver supports protected management frames (PMF) on WPA2/RSN networks,
which prevents spoofing of management frames such as deauthentication and
disassociation.
.Pp
The
.Nm
driver can be configured at runtime with
.Xr ifconfig 8
or on boot with
.Xr hostname.if 5 .
.Sh FILES
The adapter needs the following firmware file to run, which is loaded when an
interface is brought up:
.Pp
.Bl -tag -width Ds -offset indent -compact
.It Pa /etc/firmware/rtw8821c_fw.bin
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
The following example scans for available networks:
.Pp
.Dl # ifconfig urtwx0 scan
.Pp
The following
.Xr hostname.if 5
example configures urtwx0 to join network
.Dq mynwid ,
using WPA key
.Dq mywpakey ,
obtaining an IP address using DHCP:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
join mynwid wpakey mywpakey
inet autoconf
.Ed
.Sh DIAGNOSTICS
.Bl -diag
.It "urtwx0: firmware download failed"
The driver was unable to load or start the firmware needed by the adapter.
The firmware file may be missing, corrupted, or incompatible with the device.
.It "urtwx0: power-on/system init failed"
The adapter did not complete low-level hardware initialization.
The interface will remain unusable until initialization succeeds.
.It "urtwx0: could not start RX path"
The receive path could not be started.
This should not happen.
.It "urtwx0: device timeout"
A frame dispatched to the hardware for transmission did not complete in time.
The driver will stop transmitting until the interface is reset.
This should not happen.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr arp 4 ,
.Xr ifmedia 4 ,
.Xr intro 4 ,
.Xr netintro 4 ,
.Xr usb 4 ,
.Xr hostname.if 5 ,
.Xr ifconfig 8
.Sh HISTORY
The
.Nm
driver first appeared in
.Ox 8.0 .
.Sh CAVEATS
The
.Nm
driver currently supports station mode operation.
Host AP mode is not supported.
.Pp
5GHz operation is limited to non-DFS channels.
.Pp
Support for additional RTL88xx USB chipsets is not yet implemented.
